Reticular synthetic logic, in which systematic perturbation of molecular structures gives rise to predictable variation in materials properties, underpins the rational synthesis of metalorganic frameworks (MOFs).[1] Solvothermal syntheses often yield crystalline materials when metal ions that participate in facile ligand exchange chemistry $(k_{\mathrm{H}_{2}0}>{\sim}0.04\ \mathsf{s}^{-1}$ at $298~\mathsf{K}^{[2]})$ are employed,[3][4] because reversible metal–ligand $(M-L)$ bond formation enables crystallization defects to be annealed (Figure 1a). Under rapid-exchange conditions, synthesis of mixed-metal MOFs typically results in a random distribution of metal sites within the lattice.[5] Incorporation of kinetically inert metal ions as structural elements in crystalline MOFs is a synthetic challenge,[6] and as a result, kinetically inert metal ions, which display slow M– L exchange (i.e. $k_{\mathrm{H}_{2}0}<\sim0.04\ \mathsf{s}^{-1}$ at $298\textsf{K}$ , such as Cr(III), Ru(II or III), Rh(III), Ir(III), and $\mathsf{P t}(|\mathsf{I}),$ ) are rarely encountered in MOFs (Figure 1b). Notable exceptions include Cr(III)-based MIL-100,[7] MIL-101,[8] and $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ (btc $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene-1,3,5- tricarboxylate),[9] all of which were synthesized at high temperature $(\geq160^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , in presence of high concentrations of acid modulators, and obtained only as microcrystalline powders. These observations belie the inherent challenge of crystallization of coordination polymers based on slowly exchanging M–L bonds. Reductive labilization-metathesis strategies have been advanced as an approach to incorporate kinetically inert metal ions,[4] but application of these methods requires that fast ligand exchange kinetics are characteristic of one of the isolable oxidation states of the targeted ions.
Based on the requirement for M–L reversibility to access crystalline materials, the chemical diversity of MOFs is typically limited to rapidly exchanging, weak-field donors, such as carboxylates.[10] Tools that enable systematic variation of the primary coordination sphere of lattice-bound metal ions within isostructural MOF platforms are largely unavailable.[11] These limitations arise because the inherent challenge of incorporating kinetically inert ions is exacerbated for highly basic, strong-field donors, which are commonly encountered in coordination chemistry and catalysis.[12]
As part of our efforts to develop porous catalyst platforms for chemoselective intermolecular $C-H$ amination chemistry, we have been interested in developing modular synthetic methods for Ru2[II,III]-based MOFs featuring systematically variable primary coordination spheres.[13] Previous efforts, based on metallopolymerization via Sonogashira cross-coupling chemistry, provided amorphous materials.[14] Mechanochemical polymerization has been utilized for the synthesis of MOFs[15] based on $\mathsf{C u}_{2}$ -[16], $Z n_{4}0.117]$ , and $Z r_{6}$ -based[18] metal nodes with organic ligands. We hypothesized that mechanochemical polymerization of kinetically inert metalloligands with metal ions that display facile ligand exchange kinetics would provide access to crystalline heterobimetallic materials in which the distribution of metal sites would be dictated by the sequential synthetic installation (Figure 1c). Here, we report mechanopolymerization of Ru2[II,III]-based monomers featuring unprotected carboxylic acid substituents to generate a family of isostructural mixed-metal MOFs. The developed method represents a systematic strategy to generate mixed-metal MOFs, in which the metal environment and position are controlled with atomic precision.
We initiated these investigations by developing synthetic chemistry of Ru2[II,III] complexes (1–3) featuring free carboxylic acid groups (Figure 2a), which represent molecular building units of a [M3btc2] phase. Bis-protected ligands 1a and 2a were prepared by monodeprotection of the corresponding tris-esters (see Supporting Information for details). Thermally promoted ligand exchange between bis-esters 1a–3a and ${\sf R u}_{2}({\sf O A c})_{4}{\sf C I}$ or ${\mathsf{R u}}_{2}({\mathsf{O P i v}})_{4}{\mathsf{C l}}$ afforded complexes 1b–3b. Ligand substitution reactions were monitored by electrospray-ionization mass spectrometry (ESI-MS), which provided a value of $m/z$ that corresponded to the mass of $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{L}_{4}]^{+}$ (1b and 2b) or $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{L}_{4}\mathsf{C l}]\mathsf{\bar{\Phi}}$ (3b). The $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectra of carboxylate- and amidate-based $\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{L}_{4}\mathsf{C l}$ (1b and 2b) display similar paramagnetically shifted signals as the non-carboxylated analogues ${\sf R u}_{2}(\mathsf{O B}z)_{4}\mathsf{C l}$ and $\mathsf{R u}_{2}(\mathsf{H N B z})_{4}\mathsf{C l}$ , respectively. Neither 2-pyridonate-based Ru2L4Cl complex 3b nor the non-carboxylated analogue (compound S6, Supporting Information) display well-defined $\bar{^1\mathsf{H}}$ NMR signals when measured in $d_{6}$ -DMSO at $295\ \mathsf{K}.$ Global deprotection, by treatment 1b with trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) in $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ or by treatment of 2b and 3b with tetrabutylammonium fluoride (TBAF) in THF followed by acidification with HCl, afforded complexes 1– 3, which feature free carboxylic acid substituents. In each case, deprotection was monitored by IR spectroscopy. For deprotection of 1b, disappearance of the $C-H$ bending of the t-Bu group (1367 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}.$ ) was monitored; for 2b and 3b, disappearance of the $c=0$ and Si–C stretches (1720 and $840~\mathsf{c m}^{-1},$ of ester groups were monitored. The formation of 1 and 2 was also monitored by 1NMR spectroscopy.
With access to a suite of $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ complexes featuring carboxylic acid substituents, we investigated mechanopolymerization chemistry to generate porous crystalline materials. We selected ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}$ as a co-monomer because it has been commonly employed in mechanochemical syntheses of MOFs[16a, 16b] and displays rapid ligand exchange kinetics.[3a] Ball milling a 1:4 mixture of $\mathsf{R u}_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}$ (1) and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ in the presence of added MeOH $(\upeta=0.4\mathrm{-}0.8\ \upmu\mathrm{L/mg}$ ,[19] Figure S1) afforded a green-colored crystalline solid that we assign as $\mathtt{[R u_{2}C u_{4}(b t c)_{4}C l]}$ .[20] Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis indicates that the obtained material is isostructural to both $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]$ and $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ (Figures 3 and S2). For comparison, a series of control experiments were carried out: 1) Milling a mixture of H3btc and ${\sf R u}_{2}({\sf O A c})_{4}{\sf C I}$ did not afford $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ , which is consistent with the aforementioned ligand exchange kinetics at Ru (Figure S3a), and contrasts the ready formation of $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]$ upon ball-milling H3btc and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ (Figures 3 and S2); 2) milling a mixture of $\mathsf{R u}_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}$ and MeOH without $C u(O A c)_{2}\cdot H_{2}O$ did not generate $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ , which further highlights the slow ligand exchange at Ru (Figure S3b); 3) milling a physical mixture of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ and $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]$ (Figure 4a) did not lead to $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ (Figure S4), and 4) pre-formed Cu2 paddlewheels (i.e. ${\mathsf{C u}}_{2}({\mathsf{O A c}})_{4}$ ) appear to be critical for successful mechanochemical synthesis; $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ was not obtained by ball-milling $\mathsf{R u}_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}$ and $C u(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2.5H_{2}O$ (Figure S5). In addition, attempts to prepare $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ from $\mathsf{R u}_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}$ under solvothermal conditions were uniformly unsuccessful.
The synchrotron PXRD patterns of both $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]$ $\quad\ a=b=c$ $=26.36724(6)\AA$ , Figure S6a and Table S1) and $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ (a $=b=c=26.7499(9)$ Å, Figure S6b and Table S1) rwere refined in a $F m\bar{3}m$ space group, which is in accord with the literature.[9a, 21] Given the kinetic inertness of $\mathsf{R u\mathrm{-}O}$ bonds under the mechanochemical conditions, each metalloligand in $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ is expected to be connected to eight Cu paddlewheel nodes (Figure S6c), and each Cu paddlewheel node is expected to be connected to four Cu paddlewheel nodes and four Ru paddlewheel nodes. Such ordering of Ru2 and Cu2 paddlewheels would lead to a lower symmetry of $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ as compared to either $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]$ or $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ .[22] Thus, $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ (Figure S6d) was refined in an I4/mmm space group $(a=b=18.781(7).$ Å, $c=26.43(1)\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , Table S1). The monotonic lattice contraction from c axis with increasing Cu content $(26.7499(9)\mathring{\mathsf{A}}>26.43(1)\mathring{\mathsf{A}}>26.36724(6)\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ is consistent with the relative Ru–O (2.02(1) Å in ${\mathsf{R u}}_{2}({\mathsf{O A c}})_{4}{\mathsf{C l}})$ and $\mathsf{C u\mathrm{-}O}$ (1.97(2) Å in $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ bond lengths.
The metal composition of $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ was investigated by wavelength dispersive spectroscopy (WDS) and by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) of a nitric-aciddigested sample (Table S2). Both techniques provided a Ru:Cu ratio of 1:2, which is consistent with a high degree of mechanochemical polymerization and the $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ empirical formula. Complementary IR analysis also indicates essentially complete mechanopolymerization (vide infra).
$\mathsf{x}$ -ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) data for Ru and Cu (Figure S8) suggest that the $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{2}$ units in $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ retain the Ru2[II,III] and Cu2[II,II] oxidation states, similar to that of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ and $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]$ . Further, Ru K-edge extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) data collected for $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ indicate that the local coordination geometry of polymerized Ru2 sites is unchanged under the mechanochemical synthesis conditions (Figure S9 and Table $\$3$ ). Specifically, the EXAFS data of $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ collected at the Ru K-edge are consistent with the presence of a Ru environment comprised of four $\mathsf{R u\mathrm{-}O}$ distances at 2.03(1) Å, 0.5 (disordered over two axial sites) $\mathsf{R u\mathrm{-}C l}$ distance at 2.40(6) Å, and one Ru–Ru distance at 2.282(9) Å, which is consistent with the molecular structure of ${\sf R u}_{2}({\sf O B z})_{4}{\sf C I}$ . Similarly, EXAFS analysis of $[\mathsf{R u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ provided Ru–O: 2.02(1) Å and Ru–Ru: 2.289(5) Å and single-crystal $\mathsf{X}$ -ray analysis of $\mathsf{R u}_{2}(\mathsf{O B}z)_{4}\mathsf{C l}^{[23]}$ provided Ru– O: 2.021(4) Å,[24] Ru–Ru: 2.290(1) Å, and Ru–Cl: 2.532(1) Å). Analysis of the Cu-edge data of $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ indicates the presence of four $\mathsf{C u\mathrm{-}O}$ vectors at 1.960(5) Å and one $\mathsf{C u\mathrm{-}C u}$ vector at 2.66(2) Å, which is consistent with the Cu2 structure of the nodes of $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]$ (EXAFS: Cu–O: 1.965(7) Å; Cu–Cu: 2.64(2) Å; single-crystal X-ray diffraction: Cu–O: 1.951(4) Å; Cu– Cu: 2.629(1) Å).[21]
IR spectroscopy indicates the presence of discrete Cu2 and Ru2 sites in $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ (Figure 4a). The IR spectrum of $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ displays signals at 1447 and $1642~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , which are consistent with COO–Cu2 stretching frequencies (1443 and $1633~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ in $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]^{[25]})$ . A signal at $1426~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ in the IR spectrum of $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ is consistent with a $\mathsf{C O O\mathrm{-Ru}}_{2}$ stretching mode and in agreement with the signal at $1432~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ observed in $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ .[9a, 26] One of the C–COO stretching from the Cu2 unit $(759\ c m^{-1}$ in $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}])$ was also observed in $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ at $755\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ . In addition, IR provides a diagnostic probe for the completeness of the mechanochemical synthesis reaction by examining the disappearance of the carbonyl stretching from free carboxylic acid groups at $1725\ c m^{-1}$ (Figure S10b).
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, Figure S12) of $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ revealed this material is thermally stable to $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which is comparable to the thermal stability of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ (Figure S12). Based on these observations, $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ was activated by exhaustive methanol exchange followed by heating at $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under vacuum prior to gas sorption analysis. The permanent porosity of $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ was characterized by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $77~\mathsf{K}$ (Figure S13a and Table S4). $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ has a measured BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $1051~\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathfrak{g}$ (Langmuir surface area is $1433~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g})$ . In comparison, $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ displays a BET surface area of $958~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ (Langmuir surface area at 1082 $\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g})$ ). The difference in these surface areas is approximately what would be expected based on the difference in empirical formula mass between $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ and $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ (\~1.1). Both Ru-based materials display substantially lower surface areas than would be predicted based on the crystal structure (1935 and $1835{\mathsf{m}}^{2}/{\mathsf{g}}.$ . The observed surface areas are consistent with both the typical observation than mechanochemically derived materials have lower surface areas than analogous solvothermally prepared materials and the observation that porosity in mechanochemically derived materials is negatively correlated with linker size.[16a] In our synthesis, the “linker” is Ru2 complex 1, which is substantially larger than H3btc. Pore size distribution analysis through density functional theory (DFT) model is summarized in Figure S13b. The observed pores at 6.8 and $8.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ are in line with experimental pore sizes of $[\mathsf{R u}_{6}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{3}]$ (5.9 and 8.1 Å) and $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]$ (8.6 Å). The mechanochemically synthesized $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ displays a broad range of mesopores (20–35 Å), which is consistent with the formation of defects during mechanochemical synthesis.
In contrast to unsuccessful attempts to prepare $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ by solvothermal methods, solvothermal combination of either $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}(N H,O-H_{2}\mathsf{L})_{4}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{I}]$ or $\mathsf{[R u2(N,O-H_{2}L)_{4}C l]}-$ metallomonomers featuring $N,O.$ -coordination at the Ru2 site — with $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ produced highly crystalline materials (for PXRD, see Figures S14-S15). Solid state UV-vis NIR spectroscopy indicated the incorporation of both $\mathsf{C u}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ units (Figures S21-S22). The solvothermally generated $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(N H,O-L)_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ displays a BET surface area of $1580~\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ (Figure S17 and Table S6), which is higher than that of the mechanochemically generated sample $(418\quad m^{2}/{\mathfrak{g}})$ . We hypothesize that $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(N H,O-L)_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ and $\mathsf{I R u2C u4(N,O–L)4C l}]$ can be accessed solvothermally, while $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ cannot be due to enhanced stability of the molecular precursors; benzamide and 2-pyridone display are more basic $(\mathsf{p K}_{\mathsf{a}}=23.3$ and 17.0 in DMSO, respectively) and presumably less easily exchanged from the pre-formed Ru2 complexes, than that of benzoic acid $(\mathsf{p K}_{\mathsf{a}}=$ 11.1).
To evaluate the impact of systematic variation of the primary coordination sphere on the electronic properties of latticeconfined $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ sites, we examined the IR spectra of these materials following impregnation with tetracyanoethylene (TCNE). TCNE was selected as a probe of the local Ru2 chemical environment because $\mathcal{K}=\mathsf{N}$ are sensitive to the interactions with transition metal sites.[27] TCNE displays $V C=N$ at 2261 and $2228\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (Figure S26). Analysis of samples of $\mathsf{[R u_{2}C u_{4}L_{4}C l]}$ materials following exposure to $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ solutions of TCNE revealed $V C=N$ at 2230 and 2204, 2222 and 2200, and 2220 and $2200~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ , $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(N H,O-L)_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ , and $\mathsf{I R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(N,O{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{L})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ (Figure S26), respectively. The systematic shift to lower frequency is attributed to the formation of TCNE adducts of the Ru2 sites accompanied by fractional charge transfer to the coordinated TCNE ligand from the ${\sf R u}(|{\sf I})$ site (i.e. development of TCNE•‾ character). The extent of $\pi$ -backbonding, and thus $\kappa=N$ is a reflection of the electron density at the $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ site engaged with TCNE and the order of energies measured for TCNE in $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\mathsf{b t c})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ , $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(N H,O-$ $\Delta\models\mathsf{L})_{4}\mathsf{C}|]$ , and $[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\mathsf{C u}_{4}(N,O-\mathsf{L})_{4}\mathsf{C l}]$ is consistent with the relative donicities of these ligands. Importantly, treatment of an unactivated sample of $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}]$ with a $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ solution of TCNE did not result in observable $V C=N$ peaks, indicating the lack of adduct formation at the $\mathsf{C u}_{2}$ nodes (Figure S27).
In summary, we have developed reticular mechanochemistry that enables synthesis of crystalline materials with atomistic control over both the primary coordination sphere of lattice ions and the connectivity of mixed metal materials. Polymerization is accomplished by reversible $\mathsf{C u\mathrm{-}O}$ bond formation between kinetically inert Ru2-based metalloligands and kinetically labile $C u(O A c)_{2}$ . The newly obtained crystalline materials are a solidsolution of Ru2[II,III] and $\mathsf{C u}_{2}$ sites in networks that are isomorphous to the related pure Cu2-based phases. The described mechanopolymerization strategy provides an attractive approach to preparation of mixed-metal MOF materials. Unlike postsynthetic transmetallation or direct solvothermal synthesis under fast-exchange conditions, the described strategy enables synthesis of atomically precise materials under conditions in which no ion scrambling is observed. As such, these materials differ from heterobimetallic materials derived from paddlewheel units that feature two different metal ions[28] in that the current materials are solid solutions of $\mathsf{R u}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{2}$ units. Given the ubiquity of metal paddlewheel building units in MOFs, this strategy complements the current methods of preparing heterobimetallic paddlewheel-based MOFs. This highly modular synthetic strategy allows precise control over the primary coordination sphere of the Ru2[II,III] sites in solid state materials. This strategy is envisioned to pave an effective avenue to prepare porous MOF materials that feature slow-exchanged inert metal sites and mixed-metal metal nodes with defined environment and positions.
